Trace of the escrow branch:

  1. _redeemShares(sharesToRedeem, sharesOwner, isEscrowed=true, ...) reads the account's own request via
  withdrawRequestManager.getWithdrawRequest(address(this), sharesOwner) — a per-(vault, account) record, not the shared
  pool record. The pro‑rata numerator w.yieldTokenAmount and denominator w.sharesAmount are both taken from that same
  account-scoped struct, so the ratio is exactly the amount that account escrowed per share it froze. The formula is
  only "inflatable" if sharesToRedeem can exceed w.sharesAmount.
  2. Whether sharesToRedeem > w.sharesAmount is reachable is decided entirely outside this file, in two places, neither
  of which is shown here:
    - the caller in AbstractYieldStrategy that computes isEscrowed and the share count being redeemed (Notional's
  pattern is that an account with a pending request is frozen: the escrow branch is entered for the request's shares and
  entry/mint into a position with a pending request is not permitted, which is also why convertToAssets above
  unconditionally routes a pending account to getWithdrawRequestValue);
    - withdrawRequestManager.finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest({... withdrawYieldTokenAmount: yieldTokensBurned,
  sharesToBurn: sharesToRedeem}), which is the function that actually debits the account's request and releases tokens
  from the shared cooldown position. The manager necessarily decrements w.sharesAmount by sharesToBurn and
  w.yieldTokenAmount by withdrawYieldTokenAmount (or deletes the request when fully consumed); any sharesToBurn >
  w.sharesAmount or withdrawYieldTokenAmount > w.yieldTokenAmount underflows and reverts in 0.8.x checked arithmetic.
  That is precisely the bookkeeping this finding assumes is absent, and it is in code the reviewer never traced.
  3. The integer division w.yieldTokenAmount * sharesToRedeem / w.sharesAmount truncates down, so for any sharesToRedeem
  <= w.sharesAmount the caller is under-credited, never over-credited — rounding favors the pool, not the redeemer.
  There is no rounding-direction defect here.
  4. The tokenizeWithdrawRequest path in _postLiquidation shows the intended way a request's shares move between
  accounts (liquidator receives a tokenized slice), i.e. the manager tracks shares against requests explicitly rather
  than letting a raw balance drive the ratio — further evidence that "receive shares while a request is pending" is
  mediated by the manager, not by the naked share balance.

  So the claimed over-release requires sharesToRedeem > w.sharesAmount to survive the manager's own accounting, and
  nothing in the presented code demonstrates that it does; the arithmetic in the manager overwhelmingly points the other
  way. The exploit step that would break the invariant cannot be pointed at in shown code, and the tie-break rule
  applies.

  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/staking/AbstractStakingStrategy.sol:_redeemShares:100|class=C21|The pro-rata uses the account's
  own per-(vault,account) w.yieldTokenAmount/w.sharesAmount and truncates down, and any sharesToRedeem > w.sharesAmount
  must still pass finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest, which debits the request in checked arithmetic and reverts on
  underflow — the claim rests on unshown manager code the reviewer never traced.
